      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "> **Fine motor skills:** Fine motor skills are smaller, more controlled movements, especially of the hands and fingers. They also involve the eyes and hands working together.\n\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Reaching for a toy\n- Grasping a rattle\n- Picking up small pieces of food\n- Holding a spoon\n- Turning pages\n- Scribbling with a crayon\n- Drawing\n- Building with blocks\n- Buttoning clothes\n- Using child-safe scissors with supervision\n\n\nFine motor skills matter because they help children become more independent. A child who can hold a spoon can try to feed themselves. A child who can hold a crayon can draw and later learn to write. A child who can turn pages can explore books more independently.\n\n\nFine motor skills also support learning. When children build, draw, sort, pour, and manipulate objects, they are strengthening hand control, concentration, problem-solving, and coordination.",
