      "title": "8. The Role of Play",
      "order": 8,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "Play is one of the most powerful ways children develop physically. Through play, children practise balance, strength, coordination, problem-solving, and confidence.\n\n\n> **Play:** WHO describes play as voluntary, enjoyable activity that can be solitary or social and may involve objects. Young children build and strengthen developmental skills through playful interaction with people and objects.\n\n\n### Recommended Play: Babies\n\nBabies learn through safe floor movement, reaching, grasping, kicking, rolling, and tummy time.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n- Place a safe toy just within reach.\n- Let the baby kick a soft ball.\n- Talk and sing during tummy time.\n- Offer safe objects to hold and explore.\n\n\n### Recommended Play: Toddlers\n\nToddlers need chances to walk, carry, push, pull, climb safely, and practise hand skills.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n- Let the child carry a small plastic cup to the table.\n- Provide a ball for rolling or kicking.\n- Encourage safe climbing on low steps with supervision.\n- Offer crayons and paper for scribbling.\n\n\n### Recommended Play: Preschool children\n\nPreschool children benefit from running, jumping, balancing, throwing, catching, dancing, and active games.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n- Play “stop and go” or simple chasing games.\n- Throw and catch a soft ball.\n- Dance to music.\n- Walk on a low, safe line drawn on the ground to practise balance.\n\n\n> **Key Principle:** Children do not need costly equipment. A ball, cups, safe containers, paper, crayons, music, and a safe outdoor space can provide many learning opportunities.",
      "coachNotes": "Guide the student through the role of play, emphasizing evidence-based movement milestones, safe play, and early pediatric red flags."
