      "title": "10. When Should a Parent Be Concerned?",
      "order": 10,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "A developmental concern does not automatically mean something is wrong, but it is worth discussing with a qualified professional.\n\n\nSpeak with a doctor, nurse, or other qualified child-health professional if you notice:\n\n- The child is not making expected progress in movement over time.\n- The child has significant difficulty with movement compared with their usual abilities.\n- The child persistently uses one side of the body much more than the other.\n- The child loses a skill they previously had, such as no longer walking or sitting as before.\n- The child has major difficulty with walking, balance, coordination, or using their hands.\n- The child seems unusually stiff, floppy, weak, or in pain during movement.\n- The parent or caregiver has a concern that continues or seems unusual.\n\n\n> **Clinical Evidence:** The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends ongoing developmental monitoring and standardized developmental screening at set ages, with additional assessment whenever parents or professionals have concerns.\n\n\n> **Safety Guidance:** Do not try to diagnose a child using the internet. A single late skill does not automatically mean a disorder. What matters is the overall pattern of the child’s development, whether progress is continuing, and whether skills are being lost.",
