      "title": "11. Practical Case Study",
      "order": 11,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "### Case Study: Case: Amara and her two-year-old son, Musa\n\n**Setting:** Busy neighbourhood in Yaoundé\n**Participants:** Amara (Parent), Musa (Child, 2 years)\n\nAmara lives in a busy neighbourhood in Yaoundé. Her son, Musa, is two years old. At a family gathering, Amara notices that her sister’s child, who is also two, runs confidently and kicks a ball. Musa walks well but prefers to hold his mother’s hand and does not run much.\n\nAmara becomes worried. A neighbour tells her that Musa must be “delayed” because he is not running like the other child.\n\nAmara decides to observe Musa calmly for one week. She notices that he:\n\n- Walks steadily and climbs onto the sofa with help\n- Picks up small objects and puts them into a bowl\n- Holds a spoon and tries to feed himself\n- Kicks a ball when it is placed close to him\n- Enjoys dancing to music\n- Is learning new words and follows simple instructions\n\nAmara also creates more chances for movement. She lets Musa carry safe plastic cups to the table, kick a ball in a safe open space, and scribble with crayons. She reduces long periods when he is seated or restrained.\n\nAfter two weeks, Musa is beginning to take a few quick running steps when playing chase with his mother. Amara still decides to mention her earlier concern at Musa’s next health visit, especially because she wants reassurance and professional guidance.\n\n#### What this case teaches:\n\n- Children should not be judged only by comparison with another child.\n- It is helpful to observe the child’s overall abilities, not one skill alone.\n- Children need safe daily opportunities to move and practise.\n- A caregiver’s concern deserves respectful attention.\n- If a concern continues, a qualified healthcare professional should assess the child.\n\n#### Discussion questions:\n\n1. Why was it helpful for Amara to observe Musa over several days instead of deciding immediately that something was wrong?\n2. What physical skills was Musa already showing?\n3. What simple, low-cost activities could Amara use to encourage Musa’s movement?\n4. When should Amara seek professional advice about Musa’s physical development?\n5. How can caregivers support a child without comparing them with other children?",
